      "Incline changes only when you stop and step off",
      "6% maximum is a gentle hill",
      "Same confusing title as the auto version",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For walkers who want a folding treadmill with some incline for under $300 and are happy to set it before each walk.",
    description: "This T12 listing is the manual-incline version. The bullet points give three incline levels, 0%, 3% and 6%, set by hand rather than by motor, along with the same 0.5 to 7.5 mph speed range, 42.5 by 16.5-inch belt, cushioning, heart-rate sensors, 2.5 HP motor and 300 lb capacity as the auto-incline T12. The folded size is the same, 49.2 by 25.6 by 27.6 inches, and Merach again states a one-year warranty and lifetime customer support. Amazon gives the item weight as about 95 lb. It was $296.99 on 9 November 2026, about $60 less than the powered version. For someone who walks at one steady incline, manual adjustment is no real hardship and removes a motor and its wiring from the list of things that can go wrong. For interval walks that change gradient every few minutes, it is the wrong machine. As with the auto version, the title advertises “0-6/15%” incline, so check that the bullets say manual and 6% if this is the one you want.",
  },
  {
    id: "merach-novawalk-w50",
    name: "MERACH NovaWalk W50 TrekPad",
    brand: "MERACH",
    category: "Walking pad with 12% powered incline",
    badge: "Best Walking Pad",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HJTT2S6R?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41N86eQUrbL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $330 (Amazon, 9 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":330,"high":330},
    specs: "12% auto incline · 42.5 x 16.5 in. belt (bullets) · to 4 mph · 3.5 HP brushless (max) · 400 lb capacity · remote and app",
    pros: [
      "Powered 12% incline on a pad that slides under a desk",
      "400 lb rating on a heavy-duty steel frame, per the listing",
      "A 42.5-inch belt, long for a walking pad",
      "Remote and app control, so you need not bend to change speed",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Walking only: 4 mph top speed and no handrail",
      "Listing claims under 25 dB, which we would not rely on",
      "No warranty term in the bullet points we read",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Merach’s best all-round walking pad: a powered incline and a high weight rating for walking at a standing desk.",
    description: "The NovaWalk W50 is the walking pad most people mean when they search for a Merach. The listing gives a heavy-duty steel frame rated to 400 lb, a maximum 3.5 HP brushless motor, a top speed of 4.0 mph and a 12% powered incline, with a multi-layer shock-absorbing system and a 16.5 by 42.5-inch non-slip belt. An LED display shows speed, time, distance and calories, and you change settings with the remote or the Merach app. It was $329.99 on 9 November 2026. A 42.5-inch belt is long for a pad and helps taller walkers, though incline walking tends to push you towards the back. The listing’s claim of under 25 dB, “quieter than a whisper”, is not a figure we would trust for any motorised treadmill; judge noise in your own room while you can still return it. There is no handrail, so this suits steady walking at a desk rather than anyone who needs something to hold, and the bullets give no warranty term.",
  },
  {
    id: "merach-ultrawalk-w60",
    name: "MERACH UltraWalk W60 Walking Pad",
    brand: "MERACH",
    category: "Heavy-duty walking pad with powered incline",
    badge: "Best for Heavier Walkers",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H1WZJX6S?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41WPVTUITbL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $350 (Amazon, 9 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":350,"high":350},
